    "q-130544": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130544",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Given that the continuous random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[k,11k],$ where $k$ is a positive number, compute $P(0<X<6k).$",
      "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[k,11k],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10k},\\, & k\\le x\\le 11k, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X<6k) & =P(k<X<6k) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{k}^{6k}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{k}^{6k}\\frac{1}{10k}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6k-k}{10k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
