    "q-130546": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130546",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Given that the continuous random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[0,12],$ compute $P(-1\\le X\\le 3).$",
      "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[0,12],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 12, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1\\le X\\le 3) & =P(0\\le X\\le 3) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{12}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3-0}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
