    "q-96331": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96331",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Given that the continuous random variable $V$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\frac{1}{2},2],$ compute $P(-1\\le V<\\frac{1}{2}).$",
      "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $V$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(v)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le v\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $V$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\frac{1}{2},2],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(v)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}, & -\\frac{1}{2}\\le v\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1\\le V<\\frac{1}{2}) & =P(-\\frac{1}{2}\\le V<\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}f(v)\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}-(-\\frac{1}{2})}{\\frac{5}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
