    "q-96336": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96336",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Given that the continuous random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-h,5h],$ where $h$ is a positive number, compute $P(0<X\\le 3h).$",
      "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-h,5h],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6h},\\, & -h\\le x\\le 5h, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X\\le 3h) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3h}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3h}\\frac{1}{6h}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3h-0}{6h} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
