    "q-96294": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96294",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Given that the continuous random variable $U$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ compute $P(U\\ge 0).$",
      "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $U$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le u\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $U$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & -\\frac{5}{2}\\le u\\le \\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(U\\ge 0) & =P(0\\le U\\le \\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}f(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2}-0)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
