    "q-130690": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130690",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Given that the continuous random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{2}],$ compute $P(X\\ge 1).$",
      "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{2}],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & \\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 1) & =P(1\\le X\\le \\frac{9}{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{9/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{9/2}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{2}-1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
